# heroes measure [file] — the spec budget, measured (design.md §1.6).
#
# Port of archive/bootstrap-rs/heroes-cli/src/commands/measure.rs, and the reason it is in
# the port at all: design.md §1.6 names `archive/bootstrap-rs/heroes/src/measure/gate.rs`
# as the budget's live enforcer, so archiving the bootstrap without this
# would make a Part 1 sentence false and leave the ceiling defended by
# nothing (panel 085, the spec-warden's veto).
#
# §1.6 sets a MEASURED budget, and Part 10 has instructed since the first
# draft: *"count it with a real tokeniser, not by estimation — the BPE
# vocabulary contains arbitrary choices nobody predicts."* For two days the
# repository defended the ceiling with a words × 1.33 heuristic, because the
# count was assumed to need an API key. It does not (panel 011): two
# published tables, vendored and offline, do the job, and the first real
# count found the spec 33% larger than the heuristic claimed.
#
# TWO INSTRUMENTS, AND THE MAXIMUM BINDS. Neither is the tokeniser of the
# model that actually reads the spec — that one is unpublished. Two tables
# disagreeing is the honest error bar, so the report prints both and the
# verdict uses the larger.
#
# WHERE THE TABLES COME FROM, and it is the one thing the port had to decide
# for itself. The Rust resolves the repository root at COMPILE time
# (`CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR`), which a Heroes program cannot do and should not
# want to: panel 085 R5 measured what that class of trick costs when it
# fails — the port read its standard library from `archive/bootstrap-rs/` at run time,
# and outside the repository every program was refused on the AUTHOR's line.
# So the tables are read relative to the working directory, and their
# absence is exit 2 in the tool's own name, never a silent empty table. That
# is honest here in a way it would not be elsewhere: what this command
# measures by default is `spec/heroes-spec.md`, a file in this repository,
# so a run outside the repository has no question to answer.

use cli/io
use cli/argv
use cli/digest
use cli/count_tokens
use cli/refresh
use cli/table as cli_table
use measure/bpe
use measure/pieces

# The file the ceiling is actually about.
constant SPEC: str
    "spec/heroes-spec.md"

constant VENDOR: str
    "vendor/tokenizers"

# design.md §1.6, raised to 8192 by author decision (2026-09-12), to make room
# inside the spec for the grammar's productions — panel 133 found that every
# free-standing grammar in its survey has been archived, and that the one
# arrangement still alive is Wirth's own, the productions beside the prose they
# govern. The raise relaxes nothing: §1.6's payment rule is unconditional at
# every level, so a block of productions owes what a sentence owes.
#
# It was 6144 by author decision (2026-09-09), and THAT is where THE NUMBER
# STARTED CARRYING ITS INSTRUMENT, because the one before it did not. It was 4096
# taken as the maximum over two VENDORED tokenisers, and the larger of those
# is `cl100k_base`, which is OpenAI's: measured 2026-09-09 against
# `POST /v1/messages/count_tokens`, the spec was 5094 real tokens on
# `claude-opus-5` against a stated 4096, so the document had been 998 OVER a
# ceiling it was told it was 40 under (`docs/measurements/023`). One pinned
# model id binds and moves only by author decision (panel 123 R3); the spread
# over other ids is published beside it and gates nothing, because Opus 4.7
# counts the same content as Opus 5 and wraps it differently, so a maximum
# over a SET is not a property of the document.
constant CEILING: i64
    8192

# THE RECORD: the reader's own count, refreshed by hand and never by a check
# (panel 123 R1 and R5). The gate may not touch the network — design.md Part
# 11 metric 1 forbids it in as many words, and the repository being public
# means GitHub passes no secrets to a fork's pull request, so a key-gated
# check is structurally red on every fork PR forever. So the number is
# recorded, with the model that produced it and the day it was taken, and
# `heroes measure --refresh` is what goes and asks. It is the shape Bazel's
# `sha256`, pip's hash-checking mode and Nix's fixed-output derivations all
# take, and Go's `toolchain` line is the precedent for writing the
# instrument's name beside the pinned data, *"for reproducibility"*.
#
# THE DIGEST IS WHAT MAKES A STALE RECORD LOUD. It is the spec's content
# hash, and when it does not match the file in hand the verdict is WITHHELD
# rather than computed — because a headroom sentence against a stale count is
# the exact sentence that cost this project 998 tokens.
constant REAL_TOKENS: i64
    5716

constant REAL_MODEL: str
    "claude-opus-5"

constant REAL_TAKEN: str
    "2026-09-12"

constant SPEC_DIGEST: str
    "35968b435ed519d7"

# THE SOFT LINE IS RETIRED, and it is the named removal that pays for the
# raise above (panel 123 R7). It was 2000, set when the spec measured 2231 on
# the vendored instrument; on the real one the spec has been above it since
# v0, so it was never once slack and its `else` branch was unreachable in
# practice. §1.6 forbade rescaling it with the ceiling, which left it drifting
# further from meaning anything at every raise. What replaces it is stricter:
# the payment rule is now UNCONDITIONAL — every amendment owes a named removal
# or a registered prediction naming an instrument that exists today (panel
# 012, as amended by panel 046), at every level.

# WHAT THE CEILING ALREADY OWES, and it is defect 017's repair (filed and
# closed 2026-09-08, found by panel 118's spec-warden about its own
# instrument). §1.6's 4096 is not all spendable: panel 030 R3 mortgages a
# floor against it for file I/O, `args()` and `exit(code)` — the FFI's own
# clause, which the spec must be able to state — and
# `tests/harness/suite_spec.hero` goes red at `SPEC_TOKENS + FFI_FLOOR >=
# CEILING`. Printing `CEILING - highest` therefore told a session it had 60
# tokens it did not have, and a session that trusts the instrument it is
# told to trust would find out from a red suite instead. The same number in
# two places, one printed and one checked, and the harness asserts this
# line says what it computes.
constant FFI_FLOOR: i64
    60

# THE OPERATING CONTRACT IS WEIGHED ON THIS SCALE AND NOT AGAINST THIS
# CEILING, which is defect 017's own class rather than its witness: the
# spec's number was 60 too generous, and this file's was a BREACH that is
# not one. `tests/harness/suite_spec.hero` holds `CLAUDE.md` to
# `CONTRACT_CEILING`, deliberately a ceiling and not a pin, because the
# contract is amended by author instruction and often by a sentence. A
# tool that shouts BREACH at a file 111 tokens inside its own limit
# invites the one repair that is always wrong here — cutting a rule to
# make a number smaller.
constant CONTRACT: str
    "CLAUDE.md"

constant CONTRACT_CEILING: i64
    5500

record Instrument
    file: str
    name: str
    json: bool

record Reading
    instrument: str
    vocabulary: i64
    tokens: i64

function instruments() -> [Instrument]
    return [
        Instrument(file: "claude-legacy.json", name: "claude-legacy", json: true)
        Instrument(file: "cl100k_base.tiktoken", name: "cl100k_base", json: false)
    ]

function run(inv: argv.Invocation) -> cli_table.Exit
    path = inv.file.default(SPEC)
    text = read_file(path)

    if text.is_err()
        io.eprint(text: "error: cannot read `" + path + "`")
        return .failed_exit

    if argv.has(inv, spelling: "--refresh")
        return run_refresh(path: path, text: text.must())
    parts = pieces.pieces_of(text.must())
    readings: [Reading] @ []

    for one in instruments()
        table_path = VENDOR + "/" + one.file
        raw = read_file(table_path)

        if raw.is_err()
            io.eprint(
                text: "error: cannot read `" + table_path + "` — `measure` reads its vendored tables from the repository root, and the file it measures by default lives there too"
            )
            return .failed_exit
        ranks: bpe.Ranks @ bpe.empty()

        if one.json
            ranks @ bpe.from_claude_json(raw.must())
        else
            ranks @ bpe.from_tiktoken(raw.must())

        if bpe.size(ranks) == 0
            io.eprint(text: "error: `" + table_path + "` parsed to an empty table")
            return .failed_exit
        total: i64 @ 0

        for piece in parts
            total @ total + bpe.count_piece(r: ranks, piece: piece)

        readings @ readings.push(Reading(instrument: one.name, vocabulary: bpe.size(ranks), tokens: total))

    return reported(path: path, here: digest.digest(text.must()), readings: readings)

# The report, byte for byte the bootstrap's: two readings, the binding
# number, the spread, and the verdict against the ceiling. A breach is
# exit 1 — the input has something wrong with it — while an unreadable file
# is exit 2, the tool unable to run (panel 016's contract).
function reported(path: str, here: str, readings: [Reading]) -> cli_table.Exit
    print(path)
    highest: i64 @ 0
    lowest: i64 @ -1

    for one in readings
        print(
            "  " + padded(text: one.instrument, width: 16) + " " + aligned(number: one.tokens, width: 6) + "   (" + one.vocabulary.to_str() + " ranks)"
        )

        if one.tokens > highest
            highest @ one.tokens

        if lowest == -1 || one.tokens < lowest
            lowest @ one.tokens

    if lowest == -1
        lowest @ 0
    spread = highest - lowest
    percent: i64 @ 0

    if highest > 0
        percent @ spread * 100 / highest
    print(
        "  " + padded(text: "maximum", width: 16) + " " + aligned(number: highest, width: 6) + "   a lower bound, not the reader's tokeniser"
    )
    print(
        "  " + padded(text: "spread", width: 16) + " " + aligned(number: spread, width: 6) + "   between the two vendored tables (" + percent.to_str() + "%) — the gap between two REAL tokeniser generations was 967"
    )

    if path == SPEC
        if here != SPEC_DIGEST
            print("")
            print("STALE: the recorded count is for " + SPEC_DIGEST + " and this file is " + here + ".")
            print("Run `heroes measure " + path + " --refresh` and paste what it prints.")
            print("No verdict is given against a stale count: that is the sentence that")
            print("spent 998 tokens nobody had (docs/measurements/023).")
            return .diagnostics_exit
        print(
            "  " + padded(text: "real", width: 16) + " " + aligned(number: REAL_TOKENS, width: 6) + "   " + REAL_MODEL + ", " + REAL_TAKEN + " — the binding number"
        )
    print("")

    ceiling: i64 @ CEILING
    judged: i64 @ highest

    if path == SPEC
        judged @ REAL_TOKENS

    if path == CONTRACT
        ceiling @ CONTRACT_CEILING

    if judged > ceiling
        print("BREACH: " + judged.to_str() + " over the " + ceiling.to_str() + "-token ceiling (design.md §1.6).")
        print("An amendment must be net-negative, or the ceiling moves — with a")
        print("measurement, a panel, and a named alternative (panel 012).")
        return .diagnostics_exit

    print("An addition needs a named removal or a pre-registered falsifiable")
    print("prediction (panel 012). " + room(path: path, highest: judged))
    return .ok_exit

# How much of the ceiling is left, and how much of THAT is spendable. Only
# the spec carries a mortgage today, so only the spec is told about one:
# saying it of every file would be a claim about a ceiling those files do
# not share.
function room(path: str, highest: i64) -> str
    if path == CONTRACT
        return "Headroom: " + (CONTRACT_CEILING - highest).to_str() + " against the contract's own " + CONTRACT_CEILING.to_str() + ", which is a ceiling and not a pin: the repair for crossing it is to move a story into docs/records/contract/case-law.md, never a bigger number."
    left = CEILING - highest

    if path != SPEC
        return "Headroom: " + left.to_str() + "."

    # THE SUM THE CHECK COMPARES, and not a subtraction of my own: the
    # harness goes red at `REAL_TOKENS + FFI_FLOOR >= CEILING`, so a
    # "usable room" figure computed here is off by one the moment that
    # `>=` is read as `>`. It was, in the first version of this line,
    # 2026-09-08. Stating both sides of the comparison leaves the reader
    # no arithmetic to get wrong.
    return "Headroom: " + left.to_str() + " against the " + CEILING.to_str() + " ceiling — but the FFI floor mortgages " + FFI_FLOOR.to_str() + " of it (panel 030 R3), so what is measured against the ceiling is " + (highest + FFI_FLOOR).to_str() + " and the check goes red at " + CEILING.to_str() + "."

# `--refresh`: ask the reader's own tokeniser and print the record to paste.
#
# THE ARTIFACT IS THE FOUR CONSTANTS, ON STDOUT, and the instruction that goes
# with them is on stderr — `.claude/rules/cli-surface.md`'s contract, and here
# it earns itself: a reader can redirect stdout into a file and get eight lines
# that compile.
#
# AND IT REFUSES EVERY FILE BUT THE SPEC. The record belongs to one document. A
# digest of another file is a digest nothing cross-checks, printed beside a
# count nothing judges: a measurement in the shape of one, which is the class
# this whole flag exists to close.
function run_refresh(path: str, text: str) -> cli_table.Exit
    if path != SPEC
        io.eprint(
            text: "error: `--refresh` refreshes the record for `" + SPEC + "`, and `" + path + "` has none — no ceiling judges it and no check keeps it honest (panel 123 R5)"
        )
        return .failed_exit
    lines = refresh.refreshed(text)

    if lines.is_err()
        io.eprint(text: "error: " + refresh.why_lines(lines))
        return .failed_exit

    for row in lines.must()
        print(row)

    io.eprint(
        text: "note: paste these into `tests/harness/suite_spec.hero` AND this file, in ONE commit — the two copies are what the harness cross-checks against what this tool prints, and the digest is of `" + SPEC + "` exactly as it stands now"
    )
    return .ok_exit

# Left-aligned in `width` columns, counted in characters.
function padded(text: str, width: i64) -> str
    out: str @ text
    count: i64 @ text.chars().len()

    while count < width
        out @ out + " "
        count @ count + 1

    return out

# Right-aligned in `width` columns.
function aligned(number: i64, width: i64) -> str
    shown = number.to_str()
    out: str @ ""
    count: i64 @ shown.chars().len()

    while count < width
        out @ out + " "
        count @ count + 1

    return out + shown

test "a reading is laid out in the bootstrap's own columns"
    readings: [Reading] = [
        Reading(instrument: "claude-legacy", vocabulary: 64995, tokens: 3440)
        Reading(instrument: "cl100k_base", vocabulary: 100256, tokens: 3512)
    ]

    # The verdict is printed, not returned as text — what this pins is the
    # arithmetic and the branch, which is where a port goes wrong.
    verdict = reported(path: "spec/heroes-spec.md", here: SPEC_DIGEST, readings: readings)
    assert cli_table.exit_code(verdict) == 0

    # And the staleness gate WITHHOLDS rather than computes: a digest that
    # does not match the record is exit 1 with no headroom sentence, which
    # is the whole point of recording one.
    stale = reported(path: "spec/heroes-spec.md", here: "beefbeefbeefbeef", readings: readings)
    assert cli_table.exit_code(stale) == 1

test "the columns are the bootstrap's widths"
    assert padded(text: "claude-legacy", width: 16) == "claude-legacy   "
    assert padded(text: "a name past the width", width: 4) == "a name past the width"
    assert aligned(number: 3440, width: 6) == "  3440"
    assert aligned(number: 0, width: 6) == "     0"
    assert aligned(number: 1234567, width: 6) == "1234567"

test "a breach is exit 1 and a clean measurement is exit 0"
    over: [Reading] = [Reading(instrument: "one", vocabulary: 1, tokens: CEILING + 1)]
    assert cli_table.exit_code(reported(path: "x", here: "unused", readings: over)) == 1
    under: [Reading] = [Reading(instrument: "one", vocabulary: 1, tokens: 10)]
    assert cli_table.exit_code(reported(path: "x", here: "unused", readings: under)) == 0

test "no operand measures the spec, and an unreadable file is the tool failing"
    # The whole pipeline, on the real tables and the real spec: this is the
    # one test here that costs a second, and it is the one that would have
    # caught every mistake in the two modules under it.
    none: str? = fail(code: "no_operand", msg: "measure was given no file")
    assert cli_table.exit_code(run(asked(none))) == 0
    named: str? = ok("no/such/file.md")
    assert cli_table.exit_code(run(asked(named))) == 2

    # And a file that is not the spec is refused BEFORE the network, which is
    # the half of `--refresh` a test can reach without a key: `.env` is not
    # here, and the refusal above happens on the path rather than on the key.
    other: str? = ok(CONTRACT)
    refused: [argv.FlagHit] = [argv.FlagHit(spelling: "--refresh", value: fail(code: "none", msg: "plain"))]
    asking = argv.Invocation(tag: .measure_cmd, file: other, program_args: [], flags: refused)
    assert cli_table.exit_code(run(asking)) == 2

# An invocation with no flags, which is what `measure` was until `--refresh`.
function asked(file: str?) -> argv.Invocation
    return argv.Invocation(tag: .measure_cmd, file: file, program_args: [], flags: [])

# The recorded model and the pinned model are two different facts and they
# must agree: `PINNED_MODEL` is who `--refresh` asks, `REAL_MODEL` is who
# answered the number in the record. A drift between them is a record
# attributed to the wrong tokeniser, which is the whole class this machinery
# exists to close.
test "the model in the record is the model the refresh asks"
    assert REAL_MODEL == count_tokens.PINNED_MODEL

# The digest was a placeholder for one build, and this is what stopped it
# staying one. It is kept rather than deleted: a record whose digest is all
# zeroes is a count of nothing, and the day somebody pastes a number without
# pasting what it was a count OF, this fires.
test "the record's digest is a real one and not the placeholder"
    assert SPEC_DIGEST != "0000000000000000"
    assert SPEC_DIGEST.len() == 16
